    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "First, the sample may not be representative of the Brazilian population, as it was carried out in a single city in a single state. Second, there is a need to study other constructs, such as body dissatisfaction, which can predict other aspects of disordered eating in this population. Third, reported weight and height may be a bias, despite being a common finding in body image and eating behavior studies. Finally, even though the EAT-26 is frequently used in Brazil, the measure is not validated for adults in the country. Moreover, the EAT-26 appears to be a more focused instrument for investigating the symptoms of disordered eating often seen in women, while recent studies have shown that disordered eating among men has its own characteristics."
    },
